<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Apple requires this file, and it is a declaration rather than a
	  description: what it claims has to match what the binary does, and the
	  answers below were each checked against the source rather than assumed.

	  The app tracks nobody. There is no analytics SDK, no crash reporter and no
	  third-party package of any kind - LearnrEngine is the only dependency and
	  it is in this repository. The seven endpoints the client calls are all
	  first-party: /auth/redeem, /me, /me/level, /play/state, /sessions,
	  /speed/runs and /content/manifest.
	-->

	<!--
	  Never. Tracking in Apple's sense is linking this app's data to data from
	  other companies' apps or sites, and nothing here leaves the LearnR API.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>

	<!--
	  What the app sends to its own server, and why.

	  A child signs in with a four-character code a parent hands over. There is
	  no name, no email address, no date of birth and no device identifier in
	  any request: the code redeems to a token, and everything after that is
	  answers to maths and English questions.

	  "Other Data" is the honest category for an attempt - which question, what
	  the child answered, whether it was right, how long it took. It is not
	  contact info, not identifiers, not usage data in Apple's sense (that
	  category is about advertising and product analytics), and there is no
	  category that fits schoolwork more closely.

	  Linked to the child's account, because that is the whole point - a parent
	  reads the report. Not used for tracking.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherDataTypes</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!--
	  Required-reason APIs. All four audited categories were grepped for across
	  the app and the engine, and this is the only one the code touches.

	  UserDefaults is NOT declared: the app uses none. The login token lives in
	  the Keychain (Keychain.swift), and the queue, the packs, the account and
	  the player snapshot are files in Application Support. Keychain access
	  inside a single app needs no entitlement and no declaration.

	  The file-timestamp APIs (C617.1), disk space (E174.1), boot time (35F9.1)
	  and active keyboards (54BD.1) are all unused - so nothing about them is
	  declared here, and this array stays empty rather than carrying a reason
	  the binary cannot justify.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
